<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Pathways to loneliness, and what keeps it going</title><!--[if gte mso 9]><xml><w:WordDocument><w:View>Print</w:View></w:WordDocument></xml><![endif]--><style>body,p,li,td{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.35;color:#17130f}h1,h2,h3{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-weight:normal;color:#8c1912}h1{font-size:20pt}h2{font-size:14pt}p.note{font-size:9pt}td,th{border:1px solid #e4dcd0;padding:3pt 5pt;font-size:9pt;vertical-align:top}table{border-collapse:collapse}</style></head><body><p style="color:#8b7f72;font-size:9pt">RUBICON REPORT</p><h1>Pathways to loneliness, and what keeps it going</h1><p class="asked"><b>Question</b> Causal mapping, in the respondents&#x27; own words, of interviews 8, 10, 18, 22, 27, 39, 41 and 43: what do these 8 young people say leads into loneliness, and what keeps it going? Which causal links, and which pathways stated within one person&#x27;s account, do the most of the 8 describe? Each link is marked as onset or maintaining, and as about the speaker&#x27;s own loneliness or about others&#x27;. Frequency means the number of the 8 people stating it. The answer is a causal map of the most-cited factors and links, with counts out of 8, for internal exploration.</p><p>Speaking of their own lives, these young people most often trace loneliness to being physically alone through circumstance (4 of 8: I08, I10, I27, I43), and to conflict or distance in the family (3 of 8: I22, I27, I43); on the way to it, low self-worth or social anxiety makes them stay away from people (3 of 8: I10, I27, I41). Fewer state of their own case an abusive or controlling partner (2 of 8: I10, I27), moving somewhere new (2 of 8: I27, I43), not fitting in (2 of 8: I18, I43), having nobody to open up to (2 of 8: I39, I43), withdrawing (2 of 8: I27, I41) and family trouble leading to withdrawing (2 of 8: I22, I27). Losing someone close (1 of 8: I22) and shallow friendships (1 of 8: I10), which several raise in general terms, are each stated of the speaker&#x27;s own life by I22 or I10 alone.</p><p>What keeps loneliness going is told mostly about other people. Loneliness leading to low mood is said of others by 4 of 8 (I08, I18, I41, I43) but of the speaker&#x27;s own case only by I27 (1 of 8). Withdrawing that deepens existing loneliness is said only of others (2 of 8: I08, I39); those who say withdrawing made them lonely (2 of 8: I27, I41) describe how it started, not how it was kept going. Of their own case, 3 of 8 describe anything that keeps their loneliness going (I10, I27, I43), against 5 of 8 who describe it of others (I08, I18, I39, I41, I43); with eight people that difference is not clear.</p>
<p>The map shows every link that at least two of the eight state, counting anyone who states it, whether of their own life or of others. The table after it splits each link by whose loneliness it describes.</p>

<table class="md"><thead><tr><th>Link</th><th>Stated by</th><th>Of own case</th><th>Of others</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Alone in daily life to loneliness</td><td>5 of 8</td><td>4 of 8: I08, I10, I27, I43</td><td>2 of 8: I39, I43</td></tr><tr><td>Loneliness to low mood</td><td>5 of 8</td><td>1 of 8: I27</td><td>4 of 8: I08, I18, I41, I43</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawing to loneliness</td><td>5 of 8</td><td>2 of 8: I27, I41</td><td>3 of 8: I08, I39, I43</td></tr><tr><td>Loss or break-up to loneliness</td><td>4 of 8</td><td>1 of 8: I22</td><td>3 of 8: I18, I41, I43</td></tr><tr><td>Low self-worth or social anxiety to withdrawing</td><td>4 of 8</td><td>3 of 8: I10, I27, I41</td><td>2 of 8: I41, I43</td></tr><tr><td>New place or life transition to loneliness</td><td>4 of 8</td><td>2 of 8: I27, I43</td><td>2 of 8: I18, I39</td></tr><tr><td>Not fitting in or excluded to loneliness</td><td>4 of 8</td><td>2 of 8: I18, I43</td><td>3 of 8: I18, I39, I41</td></tr><tr><td>Unable to open up to loneliness</td><td>4 of 8</td><td>2 of 8: I39, I43</td><td>3 of 8: I08, I39, I41</td></tr><tr><td>Family conflict or distance to loneliness</td><td>3 of 8</td><td>3 of 8: I22, I27, I43</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>Low mood to loneliness</td><td>3 of 8</td><td>2 of 8: I39, I43</td><td>1 of 8: I41</td></tr><tr><td>Shallow or fading friendships to loneliness</td><td>3 of 8</td><td>1 of 8: I10</td><td>2 of 8: I18, I43</td></tr><tr><td>Abusive or controlling partner to loneliness</td><td>2 of 8</td><td>2 of 8: I10, I27</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>Family conflict or distance to withdrawing</td><td>2 of 8</td><td>2 of 8: I22, I27</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>Not fitting in or excluded to withdrawing</td><td>2 of 8</td><td>1 of 8: I18</td><td>1 of 8: I41</td></tr><tr><td>Social media and phones to loneliness</td><td>2 of 8</td><td>none</td><td>2 of 8: I08, I10</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Every link in the table is coded as onset, except loneliness to low mood (maintaining), withdrawing to loneliness as I08 and I39 tell it of others (maintaining), low mood to loneliness as I41 tells it of others and I43 of himself (maintaining), and low self-worth to withdrawing as I10 tells it of herself and I43 of others (maintaining).</p>
<h2 id="s-pathways-into-loneliness">Pathways into loneliness</h2>
<p>Being physically alone through circumstance is the route the most people describe from their own life (4 of 8): working alone, being out of work, a bedroom at night, and being stuck at home with a baby. &quot;I started to feel really lonely, working alone in the studio&quot; <sup>1</sup>; &quot;from Monday to Friday, nine to five, there&#x27;s no one really around&quot; <sup>2</sup>; &quot;I would feel lonely, because it&#x27;s just literally where I’m physically alone&quot; <sup>3</sup>; and, for a young mother in a mother and baby unit, &quot;it does get quite lonely at times&quot; <sup>4</sup>. I39 and I43 also say it of other people (2 of 8) <sup>5</sup><sup>6</sup>. Against it, I10 adds of the same period &quot;Obviously, I was never really lonely because I&#x27;d always have somebody there with me to talk to&quot; (I10), I27 says of time on her own &quot;I feel like those are my best times really. When I’m without people.&quot; (I27), and I39 says &quot;Being alone can be a positive, but can also be a negative&quot; (I39).</p>
<p>Conflict or distance in the family leads to loneliness in the own accounts of 3 of 8, and nobody says it only of others: a mother and her boyfriend &quot;always fighting and arguing&quot; <sup>7</sup>, growing up with no siblings (&quot;And those were definitely like some like lonely times.&quot; <sup>8</sup>), and a family whose members now live separate lives: &quot;But the loneliness is comes and goes here and there, cause you got nephews and nieces and you see them, sometimes just miss them.&quot; <sup>9</sup>. For I22 and I27 the same family trouble also makes them keep away (2 of 8): &quot;I just always wanted to be with myself in my room&quot; <sup>10</sup>; &quot;I like to avoid problems. Even if that means… missing out on stuff and not seeing certain people.&quot; <sup>11</sup>. Against it, I22 says of her family not getting on, &quot;it doesn’t actually bother me anymore&quot; (I22).</p>
<p>Low self-worth and social anxiety lead to withdrawing in the own accounts of 3 of 8: dread before going out (&quot;like do not go out. Just stay inside.&quot; <sup>12</sup>), anxiety among strangers <sup>13</sup>, and guilt and anxiety that stop someone calling family she has lost touch with (&quot;I just don’t even know how to pick up the phone&quot; <sup>14</sup>). I41 and I43 also say it of others (2 of 8), including self-questioning that ends in cancelled plans <sup>15</sup>. I43, who describes that, says of himself &quot;So I never really had self-doubt like that&quot; (I43).</p>
<p>An abusive or controlling partner leads to loneliness in the own accounts of 2 of 8: for I27, a partner whose home area, where she had nobody, she moved to (&quot;I literally felt like that was a way for… his dad- my son’s dad to make me f… feel like I only had him&quot; <sup>16</sup>); for I10, services that turned her away while she was in an abusive relationship <sup>17</sup>.</p>
<p>A move or a new start leads to loneliness in the own accounts of 2 of 8: &quot;That first day at work&quot; <sup>18</sup>; &quot;it was quite lonely because I just stayed at home most of the time being because I didn&#x27;t have anyone there&quot; <sup>19</sup>; ending up &quot;just me and my son&quot; after moving <sup>20</sup>. I18 and I39 say it of others (2 of 8) <sup>21</sup>. Against it, I39 says experience has changed this for her: &quot;So I can not feel so lonely in that situation, because I know that I would sort of get to know people.&quot; (I39), and I43 says of his new job that &quot;slowly that loneliness disappears&quot; (I43).</p>
<p>Not fitting in, or being shut out, leads to loneliness in the own accounts of 2 of 8: racism at university (&quot;And that’s where I felt very lonely like three years being in a place where it’s predominantly White&quot; <sup>22</sup>), being the only one of one&#x27;s kind (&quot;you can instantly end up very lonely when there’s no one like you in the place you are&quot; <sup>23</sup>) and outgrowing a peer group whose norms one no longer shares <sup>24</sup>. I18, I39 and I41 say it of others (3 of 8) <sup>25</sup><sup>26</sup><sup>27</sup>. Against it, I18 says of how others see him, &quot;It’s not really a source of that much trouble or problems or negative feelings for me.&quot; (I18), and I43 says of the racism, &quot;it didn&#x27;t really affect me that much&quot; (I43).</p>
<p>Having nobody to open up to leads to loneliness in the own accounts of 2 of 8: holding something in when upset <sup>28</sup>, and having &quot;certain stuff you wanna talk about, you&#x27;re not comfortable with your friends or your family&quot; <sup>29</sup>. I08, I39 and I41 say it of others (3 of 8), including the expectation that men hide their emotions: &quot;men keeping their emotions pend up, makes them feel isolated cause they can’t share that with anyone&quot; <sup>30</sup>. Against it, I08 says of not being able to disclose, &quot;Personally, I&#x27;ll just kind of like, I&#x27;ll just try to get on with it&quot; (I08).</p>
<p>Some routes are stated of the speaker&#x27;s own life by a single person, though others raise them in general. Losing someone close: I22 of her dog (1 of 8): &quot;that actually made me feel lonely, ‘cause obviously my dog was a big part of my life&quot; <sup>31</sup>; I18, I41 and I43 speak of grief, the feared death of someone close and break-ups in general (3 of 8) <sup>32</sup><sup>33</sup><sup>34</sup>. Shallow friendships: I10 of herself (1 of 8): &quot;they don’t hold any longevity and I think that can allow you to feel lonely&quot; <sup>35</sup>, and she adds &quot;I’m more content with it now than I have been&quot; (I10); I18 and I43 say it of others (2 of 8). Having no partner: I43 only (1 of 8) <sup>36</sup>. Social media is said only of others (2 of 8) <sup>37</sup><sup>38</sup>; of himself, I08 says &quot;I feel like without social media, I feel like I would be depressed&quot; (I08).</p>
<h2 id="s-what-keeps-loneliness-going">What keeps loneliness going</h2>
<p>All but I22 describe something that keeps loneliness going (7 of 8), but of their own case only 3 of 8 do: I10&#x27;s guilt and anxiety about calling family she has lost touch with <sup>39</sup><sup>14</sup>, I27&#x27;s isolation getting &quot;quite depressing&quot; <sup>40</sup>, and I43&#x27;s deep thinking that &quot;can just bring it all up in your mind&quot; <sup>41</sup>. The accounts of what keeps loneliness going are otherwise told about other people (5 of 8: I08, I18, I39, I41, I43).</p>
<p>Loneliness leads to low mood, depression or brooding in what 4 of 8 say of others: &quot;I feel like when you&#x27;re lonely it’s easy for you to be depressed&quot; <sup>42</sup>; &quot;most people’s depression it’s because inside of them they feel like they are alone&quot; <sup>43</sup>. Only I27 says it of herself (1 of 8): &quot;I mean at times it could get quite depressing&quot; <sup>40</sup>; she also says &quot;I don’t find loneliness a massive problem&quot; (I27).</p>
<p>Withdrawing that deepens existing loneliness is said only of others (2 of 8): &quot;you will feel even more lonely because you&#x27;re, you&#x27;re like intentionally excluding yourself from things that could actually make you feel even, better&quot; <sup>44</sup>; &quot;for as long as you isolate yourself and you keep yourself away from people, that would only make the situation worse&quot; <sup>45</sup>. The own accounts of withdrawing leading to loneliness (2 of 8: I27, I41) are about how it starts: &quot;so you remove yourself then you feel lonely&quot; <sup>46</sup>. Against the idea that withdrawing makes things worse, I08 says of secluding himself &quot;I feel like that&#x27;s a good way of dealing with it, just do it by yourself&quot; (I08), I41 says &quot;it’s better to feel lonely than sad&quot; (I41), and I39 says &quot;sometimes people are genuinely happy in that state&quot; (I39).</p>
<p>Low mood leading back to loneliness is stated by 3 of 8: as a maintaining loop by I41 of others <sup>47</sup> and by I43 of himself <sup>41</sup>, and by I39 of herself as a start (&quot;I feel like being upset would sometimes leave you feeling a bit lonely.&quot; <sup>48</sup>). Loneliness causing withdrawal directly is told by I43 only, of others (1 of 8) <sup>49</sup>, and loneliness making it hard to open up by I08 only, of others (1 of 8) <sup>50</sup>.</p>
<h2 id="s-chains-told-by-one-person">Chains told by one person</h2>
<p>The paths in the coding join each interviewee&#x27;s own links only, never one person&#x27;s links to another&#x27;s. Some are told as one chain about the speaker&#x27;s own life: I27&#x27;s move away from friends and family to live near a controlling partner, finding travel to see people too much, and ending up &quot;just me and my son&quot; and isolated <sup>16</sup><sup>51</sup><sup>20</sup>, which she then says &quot;could get quite depressing&quot; <sup>40</sup>; I10&#x27;s failing to keep up family contact for lack of money, then guilt and anxiety that make reconnecting harder <sup>52</sup><sup>39</sup><sup>14</sup>, though she does not say this makes her lonely; and I41&#x27;s anxiety among strangers that makes him leave, &quot;which then makes you feel lonely&quot; <sup>13</sup>. Others are links told at different points in an interview and joined by the coding: I27&#x27;s dread of going out <sup>12</sup> and her feeling of not wanting to be there when with people <sup>53</sup> join low self-worth to loneliness through withdrawing, but she did not tell them as one story. The joined path from withdrawing through loneliness to low mood appears in the links of I08, I27, I41 and I43, but only in I27&#x27;s are all its steps about the speaker herself. (see “What keeps loneliness going”)</p>
<h2 id="s-limits">Limits</h2>
<p>These are the eight interviews the evaluator named, so a count means that many of these people and says nothing about how common a route is among young people. The interviews asked people to draw and talk about the experience of loneliness, so much of what they say is about loneliness in general, and some say they rarely feel it themselves: &quot;For me, I don&#x27;t really experience a lot of loneliness&quot; (I08); &quot;I don’t think I can relate to this&quot; (I39). The map&#x27;s numbers count people stating a link of anyone; the table gives own and others separately. Each count is of people who state a link, not of how strong its effect is. What reduces loneliness was not coded, so the map shows causes and loops, not remedies.</p><h2>How this was made</h2><h3>What was read</h3><p>All 48 documents, read in full: 24 male, 24 female.</p>
<h3>Coding: Links</h3><p class="small">one row for each passage that fits; 107 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>Read the interview and record every passage in which the interviewee says that one thing leads to, causes, deepens or keeps going another, where the effect is loneliness or lies on a chain the interviewee tells that ends in loneliness or in withdrawing from others. Record one row per link: the cause, the effect, the passage copied exactly, whether the link concerns how loneliness begins or how it is kept going, and whose experience it describes. Code what the interviewee says, not what the interviewer suggests, unless the interviewee agrees in their own words. Do not code remedies or things that reduce loneliness, wishes, or hypothetical statements about the speaker&#x27;s own future. Do not add steps the interviewee did not state.</blockquote></details>
<h4>Cause</h4><p>The factor the interviewee says leads to the effect.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Loneliness</th><td>The speaker names feeling lonely, alone, isolated or secluded, as a feeling or state, including feeling alone while among people.</td><td>Feeling lonely, isolated, alone, left out, &#x27;secluded&#x27;; feeling apart from a group one is with.</td><td>Being physically alone without a stated feeling of loneliness (Alone in daily life); choosing to stay away (Withdrawing from others).</td></tr><tr><th>Withdrawing from others</th><td>Keeping away from people by one&#x27;s own action.</td><td>Staying in, avoiding events, people or family, cutting or not renewing contact, going quiet or mentally absent in company, leaving a situation, not reaching out.</td><td>Being kept out by others (Not fitting in or excluded); being alone through circumstance such as working alone (Alone in daily life); finding it hard to open up or confide in people, even where the passage also names socialising (Unable to open up or be understood).</td></tr><tr><th>Low mood and rumination</th><td>Sadness, depression and the thinking that goes with it.</td><td>Feeling sad, depressed or low, depressive or anxious thoughts, overthinking, brooding at night, tiredness or lack of motivation, mental health problems.</td><td>Anxiety about other people or social situations, and self-doubt (Low self-worth and social anxiety).</td></tr><tr><th>Unable to open up or be understood</th><td>Having nobody to share feelings with, or keeping them in.</td><td>Feeling nobody understands you or you are the only one; keeping feelings or problems to oneself; finding it hard to open up, confide or disclose things about oneself; having no one to turn to or comfort you; norms against showing emotion; a dispute with the person one would normally turn to; not wanting to burden others.</td><td>A parent or family member not listening (Family conflict or distance).</td></tr><tr><th>Not fitting in or excluded</th><td>Being different from those around one, or being kept out by them.</td><td>Differences of identity, interests, personality, culture, race or religion; not following a peer group&#x27;s norms or outgrowing it; being judged, stereotyped, rejected, excluded or meeting racism; being the only one like you in a place; judging others so that one cannot relate to them.</td><td>One&#x27;s own low opinion of oneself (Low self-worth and social anxiety).</td></tr><tr><th>Family conflict or distance</th><td>Strained, broken or thin family relationships.</td><td>Fighting at home, parents not listening or putting a partner first, estrangement, unequal treatment, growing up with little family around, losing touch with family, family members drifting into separate lives.</td><td>A partner (Abusive or controlling partner, No partner, Loss or break-up).</td></tr><tr><th>Shallow or fading friendships</th><td>Friendships that lack care or depth, or that drift.</td><td>Temporary, party or status-based friends; people who do not really care; putting energy into new people rather than close friends; friends drifting apart, being busy, or missing out on friends&#x27; lives; connection that lacks something deeper.</td><td>Having no friends because of a move (New place or life transition).</td></tr><tr><th>New place or life transition</th><td>A change of place or stage that removes familiar people.</td><td>Moving area, starting a new job, college or university, leaving education, living far from one&#x27;s friends and family.</td><td>Loss of a particular person (Loss or break-up).</td></tr><tr><th>Loss or break-up</th><td>Losing someone or something close.</td><td>Death or feared death of someone close, grief, a relationship break-up, losing a pet.</td><td>Losing touch gradually (Family conflict or distance, Shallow or fading friendships).</td></tr><tr><th>No partner</th><td>Lacking a romantic partner.</td><td>Being single, not dating, not having someone to share things with as a partner.</td><td>A break-up (Loss or break-up).</td></tr><tr><th>Alone in daily life</th><td>Spending long stretches physically alone through circumstance.</td><td>Working alone, having no job, being at home alone at night, being stuck at home caring for a baby, being alone in one&#x27;s room.</td><td>Choosing to stay away from people (Withdrawing from others).</td></tr><tr><th>Low self-worth and social anxiety</th><td>Doubting oneself or fearing other people&#x27;s judgement.</td><td>Self-doubt, low self-regard, self-consciousness, low confidence, fear of rejection, dread before social events, anxiety among strangers, guilt or anxiety that stops one making contact, body image worries.</td><td>General low mood or depression (Low mood and rumination).</td></tr><tr><th>Social media and phones</th><td>Use of social media or phones as a cause.</td><td>Heavy use of social media or phones, online comparison, popularity contests, deleting accounts, phones getting in the way of conversation.</td><td>Talking to friends online as a remedy, which is not coded.</td></tr><tr><th>Money and costs</th><td>Lack of money as a cause.</td><td>Low pay, rent, travel costs.</td><td>Wealth or status attracting false friends (Shallow or fading friendships).</td></tr><tr><th>Abusive or controlling partner</th><td>A partner who abuses or controls, and the lack of help to leave.</td><td>Physical abuse, a partner isolating one from friends and family, services refusing help to leave.</td><td>A break-up (Loss or break-up).</td></tr><tr><th>Other cause</th><td>A cause that fits none of the factors above; used only at the cause end.</td><td>Substance use, failing exams, love of money, anything else.</td><td>Anything that fits a factor above.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h4>Effect</h4><p>The factor the interviewee says the cause leads to. &#x27;Other cause&#x27; is never used here.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Loneliness</th><td>The speaker names feeling lonely, alone, isolated or secluded, as a feeling or state, including feeling alone while among people.</td><td>Feeling lonely, isolated, alone, left out, &#x27;secluded&#x27;; feeling apart from a group one is with.</td><td>Being physically alone without a stated feeling of loneliness (Alone in daily life); choosing to stay away (Withdrawing from others).</td></tr><tr><th>Withdrawing from others</th><td>Keeping away from people by one&#x27;s own action.</td><td>Staying in, avoiding events, people or family, cutting or not renewing contact, going quiet or mentally absent in company, leaving a situation, not reaching out.</td><td>Being kept out by others (Not fitting in or excluded); being alone through circumstance such as working alone (Alone in daily life); finding it hard to open up or confide in people, even where the passage also names socialising (Unable to open up or be understood).</td></tr><tr><th>Low mood and rumination</th><td>Sadness, depression and the thinking that goes with it.</td><td>Feeling sad, depressed or low, depressive or anxious thoughts, overthinking, brooding at night, tiredness or lack of motivation, mental health problems.</td><td>Anxiety about other people or social situations, and self-doubt (Low self-worth and social anxiety).</td></tr><tr><th>Unable to open up or be understood</th><td>Having nobody to share feelings with, or keeping them in.</td><td>Feeling nobody understands you or you are the only one; keeping feelings or problems to oneself; finding it hard to open up, confide or disclose things about oneself; having no one to turn to or comfort you; norms against showing emotion; a dispute with the person one would normally turn to; not wanting to burden others.</td><td>A parent or family member not listening (Family conflict or distance).</td></tr><tr><th>Not fitting in or excluded</th><td>Being different from those around one, or being kept out by them.</td><td>Differences of identity, interests, personality, culture, race or religion; not following a peer group&#x27;s norms or outgrowing it; being judged, stereotyped, rejected, excluded or meeting racism; being the only one like you in a place; judging others so that one cannot relate to them.</td><td>One&#x27;s own low opinion of oneself (Low self-worth and social anxiety).</td></tr><tr><th>Family conflict or distance</th><td>Strained, broken or thin family relationships.</td><td>Fighting at home, parents not listening or putting a partner first, estrangement, unequal treatment, growing up with little family around, losing touch with family, family members drifting into separate lives.</td><td>A partner (Abusive or controlling partner, No partner, Loss or break-up).</td></tr><tr><th>Shallow or fading friendships</th><td>Friendships that lack care or depth, or that drift.</td><td>Temporary, party or status-based friends; people who do not really care; putting energy into new people rather than close friends; friends drifting apart, being busy, or missing out on friends&#x27; lives; connection that lacks something deeper.</td><td>Having no friends because of a move (New place or life transition).</td></tr><tr><th>New place or life transition</th><td>A change of place or stage that removes familiar people.</td><td>Moving area, starting a new job, college or university, leaving education, living far from one&#x27;s friends and family.</td><td>Loss of a particular person (Loss or break-up).</td></tr><tr><th>Loss or break-up</th><td>Losing someone or something close.</td><td>Death or feared death of someone close, grief, a relationship break-up, losing a pet.</td><td>Losing touch gradually (Family conflict or distance, Shallow or fading friendships).</td></tr><tr><th>No partner</th><td>Lacking a romantic partner.</td><td>Being single, not dating, not having someone to share things with as a partner.</td><td>A break-up (Loss or break-up).</td></tr><tr><th>Alone in daily life</th><td>Spending long stretches physically alone through circumstance.</td><td>Working alone, having no job, being at home alone at night, being stuck at home caring for a baby, being alone in one&#x27;s room.</td><td>Choosing to stay away from people (Withdrawing from others).</td></tr><tr><th>Low self-worth and social anxiety</th><td>Doubting oneself or fearing other people&#x27;s judgement.</td><td>Self-doubt, low self-regard, self-consciousness, low confidence, fear of rejection, dread before social events, anxiety among strangers, guilt or anxiety that stops one making contact, body image worries.</td><td>General low mood or depression (Low mood and rumination).</td></tr><tr><th>Social media and phones</th><td>Use of social media or phones as a cause.</td><td>Heavy use of social media or phones, online comparison, popularity contests, deleting accounts, phones getting in the way of conversation.</td><td>Talking to friends online as a remedy, which is not coded.</td></tr><tr><th>Money and costs</th><td>Lack of money as a cause.</td><td>Low pay, rent, travel costs.</td><td>Wealth or status attracting false friends (Shallow or fading friendships).</td></tr><tr><th>Abusive or controlling partner</th><td>A partner who abuses or controls, and the lack of help to leave.</td><td>Physical abuse, a partner isolating one from friends and family, services refusing help to leave.</td><td>A break-up (Loss or break-up).</td></tr><tr><th>Other cause</th><td>A cause that fits none of the factors above; used only at the cause end.</td><td>Substance use, failing exams, love of money, anything else.</td><td>Anything that fits a factor above.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h4>Role</h4><p>Whether the link describes how loneliness begins or how it is kept going.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Onset</th><td>The link describes how loneliness, or something on the way to it, comes about.</td><td>Circumstances, events and relationships that lead into loneliness or into withdrawing.</td><td>Links whose cause is loneliness itself or a reaction to it (maintaining).</td></tr><tr><th>Maintaining</th><td>The link keeps loneliness going or deepens it.</td><td>Links whose cause is loneliness itself; links whose cause the passage presents as arising from loneliness or isolation (such as withdrawing, low mood or self-doubt that follow being lonely); links the interviewee says make existing loneliness worse, bring it back, or stop the person leaving it, such as guilt that stops them re-contacting people they have lost touch with.</td><td>Withdrawing, low mood or anxiety that the passage presents as coming from something other than loneliness, such as family conflict, dread of going out or being a young mother (onset).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h4>Whose</h4><p>Whose loneliness the link describes.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Own</th><td>The interviewee&#x27;s own experience, past or present.</td><td>&#x27;I felt lonely&#x27;, &#x27;it made me feel lonely&#x27;, an account of the interviewee&#x27;s own life.</td><td>Statements about people in general, a friend, or a hypothetical person (others).</td></tr><tr><th>Others</th><td>Other people&#x27;s loneliness or people in general.</td><td>&#x27;You&#x27;, &#x27;people&#x27;, &#x27;they&#x27; used generally; a named friend; hypothetical cases.</td><td>&#x27;You&#x27; used plainly to tell the interviewee&#x27;s own story (own).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>What was counted</h3><ul><li>Documents by cause and effect <span class="small">(t_links)</span></li><li>Documents by cause and effect <span class="small">(t_map)</span></li><li>Documents by cause and effect <span class="small">(t_paths)</span></li><li>Documents by cause and effect and role <span class="small">(t_role)</span></li><li>Documents by role <span class="small">(t_roleonly)</span></li><li>Documents by cause and effect and whose <span class="small">(t_linkwhose)</span></li><li>Documents by role and whose <span class="small">(t_rolewhose)</span></li><li>Documents by cause and effect and role and whose <span class="small">(t_linkrolewhose)</span></li></ul>
<h3>The checks code ran</h3><ul><li>107 of 107 quotations found word for word in their document.</li><li>87 numbers in the answer, every one counted by code; 0 written by hand.</li><li>0 citations to passages that do not exist; 0 quotations not in the passage they cite.</li></ul>
<h3>The second reading</h3><p>A second coder coded the documents afresh to the same definitions without seeing the first coding. A fresh reader then ruled on every place the two differed, and checked every sentence and quotation of the answer against the documents, correcting the coding and the answer where they were wrong. Its record is <code>check.md</code>, in the run's zip with the second coder's rows.</p><p class="small">- Disagreements ruled: 11. For the rows (first coder): 5. For the second coder: 5. Unsettled by the definition (definition tightened and recoded): 1.
- Rows changed in coded/c_links.json: 9 (4 added, 5 altered), giving 107 rows. 7 changes come from the rulings and 2 from checking the report.
- Workflow changes: 2 (the opening-up boundary tightened in both the cause and effect lists; a tabulation t_linkrolewhose added, crossing link, role and whose, which the own-case counts by role need).
- Sentences changed: all 33 sentences of the original report were corrected, rewritten or cut; the corrected report has 56 sentences and a 15-row table splitting each mapped link into own and others.</p><h2>Passages cited</h2><p class="note"><sup>1</sup> I10: "I started to feel really lonely, working alone in the studio"</p><p class="note"><sup>2</sup> I43: "And that’s when you felt lonely as well because from Monday to Friday, nine to five, there&#x27;s no one really around"</p><p class="note"><sup>3</sup> I08: "I would feel lonely, because it&#x27;s just literally where I’m physically alone"</p><p class="note"><sup>4</sup> I27: "But it- it does get quite… [interviewer: mm] it does get quite lonely at times."</p><p class="note"><sup>5</sup> I39: "if they are completely by themselves and they say they don’t have a job, that could make them feel lonely as well"</p><p class="note"><sup>6</sup> I43: "if you&#x27;re at workplace sitting at a desk by yourself, you tend to you can get lonely as well"</p><p class="note"><sup>7</sup> I22: "it felt kind of lonely because obviously my mum was always with her boyfriends and they was always fighting and arguing"</p><p class="note"><sup>8</sup> I27: "And those were definitely like some like lonely times."</p><p class="note"><sup>9</sup> I43: "But the loneliness is comes and goes here and there, cause you got nephews and nieces and you see them, sometimes just miss them."</p><p class="note"><sup>10</sup> I22: "I just always wanted to be with myself in my room"</p><p class="note"><sup>11</sup> I27: "I like to avoid problems. Even if that means… missing out on stuff and not seeing certain people."</p><p class="note"><sup>12</sup> I27: "I start thinking like this is gonna be terrible, like do not go out. Just stay inside."</p><p class="note"><sup>13</sup> I41: "I need to go, I need to get out of it, I need to remove myself from the situation’, which then makes you feel lonely"</p><p class="note"><sup>14</sup> I10: "I just don’t even know how to pick up the phone, because I’m that anxious that they are, like upset with me"</p><p class="note"><sup>15</sup> I43: "This questioning definitely leads to like “Hmm, let’s cancel the date” or “let’s just not go out” and that definitely leads back to loneliness"</p><p class="note"><sup>16</sup> I27: "I literally felt like that was a way for… his dad- my son’s dad to make me f… feel like I only had him"</p><p class="note"><sup>17</sup> I10: "Say I felt really lonely then, at that point of moment I’ve, you know, under somebody could have made me not feel alone but they didn’t"</p><p class="note"><sup>18</sup> I43: "But that&#x27;s where you feel lonely. That first day at work."</p><p class="note"><sup>19</sup> I43: "it was quite lonely because I just stayed at home most of the time being because I didn&#x27;t have anyone there"</p><p class="note"><sup>20</sup> I27: "I just end up kind of just being just me and my son. So I do remember that just being a place where… I felt most isolated"</p><p class="note"><sup>21</sup> I39: "being in a new environment and meeting new people, it can make someone feel lonely at first"</p><p class="note"><sup>22</sup> I43: "And that’s where I felt very lonely like three years being in a place where it’s predominantly White"</p><p class="note"><sup>23</sup> I18: "you can instantly end up very lonely when there’s no one like you in the place you are"</p><p class="note"><sup>24</sup> I18: "you can get really lonely and isolated when you start realising that"</p><p class="note"><sup>25</sup> I18: "it’s about others rejecting you, and therefore being lonely"</p><p class="note"><sup>26</sup> I39: "this person could feel left out"</p><p class="note"><sup>27</sup> I41: "your brain’s automatically judged them and that makes you feel lonely, that makes you feel isolated"</p><p class="note"><sup>28</sup> I39: "Because sometimes when you are feeling upset to yourself about something and you just thought of holding it in and keeping it in, that can make you feel a bit lonely"</p><p class="note"><sup>29</sup> I43: "yes, I felt lonely at times, because sometimes there is certain stuff you wanna talk about, you&#x27;re not comfortable with your friends or your family"</p><p class="note"><sup>30</sup> I41: "men keeping their emotions pend up, makes them feel isolated cause they can’t share that with anyone"</p><p class="note"><sup>31</sup> I22: "But that actually made me feel lonely, ‘cause obviously my dog was a big part of my life"</p><p class="note"><sup>32</sup> I18: "the grieving process can also be the cause of experiencing loneliness"</p><p class="note"><sup>33</sup> I41: "if they go then you are gonna feel alone, you are not gonna have anyone"</p><p class="note"><sup>34</sup> I43: "So, if they break up, that loneliness can get even worse"</p><p class="note"><sup>35</sup> I10: "they don’t hold any longevity and I think that can allow you to feel lonely"</p><p class="note"><sup>36</sup> I43: "before I did feel loneliness in sense of like I didn&#x27;t have no female partner"</p><p class="note"><sup>37</sup> I08: "I think that&#x27;s why you kind of experience loneliness, because you&#x27;re so much, you’re so engrossed in technology"</p><p class="note"><sup>38</sup> I10: "And she must feel lonely because of that."</p><p class="note"><sup>39</sup> I10: "when you stop seeing what’s being to people so much, if you do suffer from anxiety or other kinds of mental health issues, it then makes it harder to approach those people because I felt guilty"</p><p class="note"><sup>40</sup> I27: "I mean at times it could get quite depressing"</p><p class="note"><sup>41</sup> I43: "So, it just sometimes we just sit down and look at, go a bit deep thinking and it can just bring it all up in your mind."</p><p class="note"><sup>42</sup> I08: "I feel like when you&#x27;re lonely it’s easy for you to be depressed"</p><p class="note"><sup>43</sup> I41: "most people’s depression it’s because inside of them they feel like they are alone"</p><p class="note"><sup>44</sup> I08: "you will feel even more lonely because you&#x27;re, you&#x27;re like intentionally excluding yourself from things that could actually make you feel even, better"</p><p class="note"><sup>45</sup> I39: "for as long as you isolate yourself and you keep yourself away from people, that would only make the situation worse"</p><p class="note"><sup>46</sup> I41: "so you remove yourself then you feel lonely"</p><p class="note"><sup>47</sup> I41: "this feeling in your head will always drag you back into the darkness"</p><p class="note"><sup>48</sup> I39: "I feel like being upset would sometimes leave you feeling a bit lonely."</p><p class="note"><sup>49</sup> I43: "You can end up being very introvert or you might be negative just shutting people out because you experience loneliness"</p><p class="note"><sup>50</sup> I08: "they will find it difficult to kind of socialize and kind of open up if they&#x27;re in, experiencing loneliness for a long time"</p><p class="note"><sup>51</sup> I27: "So I always had to travel like an hour and a half to see people"</p><p class="note"><sup>52</sup> I10: "I was literally living on 60 pound a week, which made it impossible to go home"</p><p class="note"><sup>53</sup> I27: "even if you are with people, you can still feel like you don’t wanna be there. Yeah, I’d definitely say that’s a personal experience"</p><h2>What next</h2><p>Treat this report as a draft. Before it is final, make sense of it with the people it concerns, such as programme staff and participants: whether the findings ring true, what they leave out, and what to do about them. Their responses can go back into a revised answer.</p><p><b>causal-mapping-in-the-respondents-own-words-of-2026-10-07.zip</b>, saved beside this report, holds the whole run: the documents, the coded passages and the workflow that recounts every number. Keep it, or pass it on to people allowed to read the documents.</p><p>Causal Map also runs workshops and consultancy on analysing qualitative evidence for evaluation: <a href="https://causalmap.app/?utm_source=rubicon-plugin&amp;utm_medium=report">causalmap.app</a>.</p></body></html>